{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"The key observation is that both `Iff`s relate propositions that are independently always true, so prove four standalone facts and assemble the equivalences by ignoring the hypotheses in each direction.\n\n1. `LSeries.convolution f g 0 = 0` follows from the convention that sequences are converted to arithmetic functions vanishing at zero; this is exactly the zero-value property of Dirichlet convolution (`LSeries.convolution_map_zero`). [Mathlib source](https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/master/Mathlib/NumberTheory/LSeries/Convolution.lean)\n\n2. The upper central series is an ascending central series by its recursive definition (`Subgroup.upperCentralSeries_isAscendingCentralSeries`).\n\n3. A plateau in the upper central series persists: since each term is obtained by applying the same upper-central-step operator,  \n   \\(U_a=U_{a+1}\\) makes \\(U_a\\) a fixed point. Induction from \\(a\\le b\\) gives \\(U_a=U_b\\); this is `Subgroup.upperCentralSeries.eq_ge_of_eq_succ`. [Mathlib source](https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/master/Mathlib/GroupTheory/Nilpotent.lean)\n\n4. Represent the positive Fitting component as\n   \\[\n   \\bigvee_{x\\in L}\\bigcap_j \\operatorname{range}(\\rho(x)^j).\n   \\]\n   An element of the \\(x\\)-component can, for every \\(k\\), be written as \\(\\rho(x)^k m\\); a \\(k\\)-fold Lie action lies in the \\(k\\)-th lower central term. Hence every component, and therefore their supremum, lies in every lower-central term. Taking the infimum gives `LieModule.posFittingComp_le_iInf_lowerCentralSeries`. Nilpotence of \\(L\\) is the essential assumption ensuring these Fitting components form Lie submodules; no Noetherian or Artinian hypothesis is needed for this one-sided inclusion. [Mathlib source](https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/master/Mathlib/Algebra/Lie/Weights/Basic.lean)"}}
